# Regression test: `terminate` on a job that's a *statement inside a closure/block
# body* (not just a single bare top-level job) must actually stop that closure from
# advancing to its next statement -- not just flip the terminated statement's own
# status flag while the closure sails on regardless.
#
# This is deliberately narrower than, and not already covered by, `tests/terminate.
# crush` (which confirms a plain backgrounded `sleep` stops almost immediately, purely
# via wall-clock timing) or the `vt_terminate_*` tests (which confirm `jobs` correctly
# reports a terminated job's *status*). Both of those were already fine before the fix
# this test exists for. The bug here was specifically about `Closure::eval_inner`'s own
# sequential-statement loop silently treating a terminated statement as having
# succeeded anyway, letting the closure run its *next* statement -- confirmed directly
# by backgrounding a two-statement block (`{sleep ...; echo SHOULD_NOT_APPEAR} &`),
# terminating it while the long `sleep` is still running, and observing that the
# `echo` afterward still ran sometimes anyway.
#
# Root cause: `ThreadStore::join_job` (the mechanism `Closure::eval_inner` used to rely
# on, alone, to decide whether to advance to the next statement) is not race-free in
# two independent ways -- see `JobHandle::termination_requested`'s own doc comment in
# `src/lang/state/handles.rs` for both in full. The one actually observed causing this
# specific failure: `join_job` first collects the ids of every thread registered under
# the just-terminated statement's job, then joins each separately -- and in the gap
# between those two steps, `ThreadStore::reap` (called by *any* new job being created
# anywhere in the system, not just this closure's own worker thread -- here, this very
# script's own subsequent top-level `jobs`/`sleep` statements, running concurrently
# with the backgrounded block) can win the race to remove and join that same thread
# first, leaving `join_job`'s own attempt with nothing left to find -- which
# `ThreadStore::join_one` treats the same as "that thread finished cleanly", silently
# discarding the statement's real `Err` and letting the loop advance. Not reliably
# reproducible on every single run (it depends on exactly when the racing `reap()` call
# lands), but reproduced directly, repeatedly, across dozens of runs before the fix
# below existed; after it, 30 consecutive runs all showed the correct behavior.
#
# Fixed by having `Closure::eval_inner` additionally check `JobHandle::
# termination_requested` (not the generic `status` field -- see that method's own doc
# comment for why the distinction matters, including a real regression this task's
# first attempt introduced and then fixed: `tests/signal_death_inside_background_
# closure_does_not_abort.crush`) after `join_job` returns, rather than trusting
# `join_job`'s own success/failure alone. `GlobalState::terminate` sets that flag
# synchronously, independent of either join race, so checking it directly is
# race-free.
#
# The extra `jobs` calls and settle delays below are deliberate, not incidental, and
# somewhat fragile as a result -- each `jobs` call only matters here because it's
# itself a new job, giving `ThreadStore::reap` another concurrent opportunity to race
# against the backgrounded block's own join (see the root-cause paragraph above);
# removing them makes the *unfixed* code reproduce this far less reliably.
{sleep 100s; echo SHOULD_NOT_APPEAR} &
sleep 200ms
jobs
terminate 0
sleep 800ms
jobs
echo MARKER
